

            PolyPascal-86 Supplementary Documentation
                  IBM PC Turtlegraphics Package

                       Copyright (C) 1986
                   PolyTech International A/S



On  your  PolyPascal distribution disk you will find three  files 
called GRAPHICS.PAS,  GRAPHICS.BIN and GRAPDEMO.PAS. They contain 
a  complete set of Turtlegraphics routines for use in your  Poly-
Pascal  programs enabling you to use medium and  high  resolution 
graphics.

The Turtlegraphics package supports four video adapters:

    IBM Color/Graphics Adapter.
    IBM Enhanced Graphics Adapter.
    Hercules Monocrome Graphics Adapter.
    Olivetti Graphics Adapter.

If  your system is not equipped with one of these graphics  adap-
ters you cannot use the Turtlegraphics package. The adapters each 
provide different modes of operation (also called graphic modes).  
These are listed below.

IBM Color/Graphics Adapter:

    320 x 200 color graphics, 4 colors.
    320 x 200 monochrome graphics.
    640 x 200 monochrome graphics.

IBM Enhanced Graphics Adapter:

    Three modes compatible with the IBM Color/Graphics Adapter.
    320 x 200 color graphics, 16 colors.
    640 x 200 color graphics, 16 colors.
    640 x 350 color graphics, 16 colors.
    640 x 350 monochrome graphics.

Hercules Monocrome Graphics Adapter:

    720 x 348 monochrome graphics.

Olivetti Graphics Adapter:

    Three modes compatible with the IBM Color/Graphics Adapter.
    640 x 400 monochrome graphics.

To  include the Turtlegraphics package in a  program,  place  the 
following  compiler directive at the very beginning of the  prog-
ram:

                          {$I GRAPHICS}

This will include the file called GRAPHICS.PAS in the compilation 
of your source text.  Alternatively you can copy the entire GRAP-
HICS.PAS file into your source text. GRAPHICS.PAS contains a list 
of EXTERNAL procedure and function declarations.  The actual code 
for  each  routine is read from a file called  GRAPHICS.BIN.  The 
Turtlegraphics  package  is written in machine code  to  optimize 
execution speed. 

GRAPDEMO.PAS  is  a pascal source text demonstrating how  to  use 
some of the Turtlegraphics routines.

The  Turtlegraphics  package  is based on  the  "turtle"  concept 
devised  by  S.  Papert and his coworkers  at  the  Massachusetts 
Institute of Technology (MIT). To make graphics easy for those of 
us who might have difficulty understanding cartesian coordinates, 
Papert  et al.  invented the idea of a "turtle" who could walk  a 
given  distance and turn through a specified angle while dragging 
a pen along.  Very simple algorithms in this system (which  could 
be called "relative polar coordinates") can give more interesting 
images  than an algorithm of the same length in cartesian coordi-
nates.

The  following procedures and functions are provided by the  Tur-
tlegraphics package:

    crtmode             Set CRT mode
    textmode            Return to text mode
    window              Define window coordinates
    background          Set background color
    palette             Set color palette
    egapalette          Set color palette for Enhanced Graphics
    pencolor            Set pen color
    colormode           Define color mapping
    fillscreen          Fill screen
    aspect              Set aspect ratio
    turtlehome          Move turtle to home position
    moveto              Move turtle
    moveby              Move turtle relative
    move                Move turtle by distance
    lineto              Draw line
    lineby              Draw line relative
    line                Draw line by distance
    turnto              Turn turtle absolute
    turn                Turn turtle relative
    plotat              Plot point
    plotby              Plot point relative
    paint               Paint area
    arc                 Draw arc
    circle              Draw circle
    pattern             Define fill pattern
    rectfill            Fill rectangle with pattern
    getpic              Get picture image from screen
    putpic              Put picture image to screen
    point               Return color of dot
    turtlex             Return turtle X-coordinate
    turtley             Return turtle Y-coordinate
    turtleang           Return turtle angle
    workpage            Set working page
    disppage            Set display page
    copypage            Copy between pages
    writestring         Write text to graphics screen
    charstyle           Set character style
    charsize            Set character size
    charfont            Set character font

Each routine is described in detail below.

The Turtlegraphics routines operate on integer coordinates. Thus, 
the  coordinate range is -32768..32767.  The graphics screen is a 
rectangle with the origin (x,y) = (0,0) at the LOWER LEFT corner. 
The  upper right corner has the  coordinates  (XMAX,YMAX),  where 
XMAX  and YMAX are the horizontal and vertical resolutions of the 
screen less one.

All Turtlegraphics routines operate within a window.  By  default 
the  window  is set to the entire graphic image,  but  there  are 
occations when it is handy to use only part of the screen,  while 
safeguarding the rest from accidental use.  For this purpose, the 
Turtlegraphics  package allow you to define any rectangle on  the 
screen  as  the  window.

When you specify the coordinates of a window,  (0,0)  corresponds 
to the lower left corner of the screen. All other coordinates are 
relative  to the window,  and (0,0) corresponds to the lower left 
corner  of the window.  When an object is drawn using any of  the 
Turtlegraphics routines, it is automatically clipped so that only 
the  portion which lies within the current window  is  displayed. 
Points whose coordinates are not within the current window,  even 
those  points that would not be on the screen at all,  are  legal 
but ignored.

It is possible to mix text and graphics on the screen. By default 
the  8x8  font  located  in the ROM-BIOS is  used  for  character 
drawing.  This  font  gives you 40 x 25 characters in 320  x  200 
mode, 80 x 25 characters in 640 x 200 mode, 80 x 43 characters in 
640 x 350 mode, and 80 x 50 characters in 640 x 400 mode. You may 
yourself define and install other fonts.

The IBM Color/Graphics Adapter and the Olivetti Graphics  Adapter 
provide only one display page.  The IBM Enhanced Graphics Adapter 
provides up to 8 display pages depending on the mode selected and 
the  amount of memory it has installed.  The Hercules  Monochrome 
Graphics  Adapter provides one or two display pages depending  on 
the mode selected.

Before  calling any of the Turtlegraphics routines you must  call 
the crtmode procedure to select a graphics mode.  If you call one 
of  the routines before calling crtmode,  the package performs  a 
fake division by zero to interrupt the program.  You may then use 
PolyPascal's FIND command to locate the graphics call that caused 
the error.


Function:   Set CRT mode.

Header:     PROCEDURE crtmode(m: integer);

Remarks:    This procedure is used to select a graphics mode. The 
            following modes are available:

              0 = 40 x 25 Black and White.
              1 = 40 x 25 Color.
              2 = 80 x 25 Black and White.
              3 = 80 x 25 Color.
              4 = 320 x 200 Color Graphics.
              5 = 320 x 200 Monochrome Graphics.
              6 = 640 x 200 Monochrome Graphics.
              7 = 80 x 25 Black and White (Monochrome Display).
              8 = 720 x 348 Monochrome Graphics on Hercules.
              9 = 720 x 348 Monochrome Graphics on Hercules.
             13 = 320 x 200 Enhanced Color Graphics.
             14 = 640 x 200 Enhanced Color Graphics.
             15 = 640 x 350 Enhanced Monochrome Graphics.
             16 = 640 x 350 Enhanced Color Graphics.
             64 = 640 x 400 Olivetti Monochrome Graphics.
             72 = 640 x 400 Olivetti Monochrome Graphics.

            The  IBM Color/Graphics Adapter supports  modes  0-6. 
            The  IBM Enhanced Graphics Adapter supports modes 0-7 
            and  13-16 (note that mode 16 works properly only  on 
            adapters  with  the Graphics  Memory  Expansion  Card 
            installed).  The Hercules Monochrome Graphics Adapter 
            supports modes 8 and 9. The Olivetti Graphics Adapter 
            supports modes 0-7, 64 and 72.

            Mode  8 corresponds to the FULL Hercules system  with 
            two  display pages.  Mode 9 corresponds to  the  HALF 
            Hercules system with only one display page. This mode 
            is  used when you have an IBM Color/Graphics  Adapter 
            installed in addition to the Hercules Adapter.

            Do  not  try  to select a graphics mode that  is  not 
            supported by your graphics adapter(s).

            When crtmode is called the window is reset to include 
            the  entire display and the pen color is set  to  the 
            highest possible value (1, 3, or 15) which is normal-
            ly  white.  Furthermore the turtle is moved to  (0,0) 
            and turned to 0.

            All of the routines described in the following do not 
            operate in modes 0-3 and 7.


Function:   Return to text mode.

Header:     PROCEDURE textmode;

Remarks:    This  procedure resets the display to text  mode.  It 
            selects CRT mode 1,  3 or 7 according to the  adapter 
            switch settings in the computer.


Function:   Define window coordinates.

Header:     PROCEDURE window(x1,y1,x2,y2: integer);

Remarks:    The  window  is  the area on the screen that  may  be 
            manipulated  by the Turtlegraphics routines.  By  de-
            fault  the  window is the entire  screen.  Everything 
            outside  the  window  remains  unchanged  during  all 
            graphic operations until it again becomes part of the 
            window.  (x1,y1) and (x2,y2) are the opposite corners 
            of the window (no special ordering is required).  The 
            coordinates  are clipped to the physical size of  the 
            screen  if they are out of  range,  so  (0,0,999,999) 
            will always select the entire display.  The turtle is 
            moved to (0,0) and turned to 0 in the new window.


Function:   Set background color.

Header:     PROCEDURE background(c: integer);

Remarks:    This  procedure  selects the  background  color  when 
            operating in modes 4 and 5, and the border color when 
            operating in modes 13 and 14.  For all other modes, a 
            call  to  background has no effect.  c is an  integer 
            between  0 to 15 which selects a color  according  to 
            the table below:

              0 = Black.                  8 = Dark Gray.
              1 = Blue.                   9 = Light Blue.
              2 = Green.                 10 = Light Green.
              3 = Cyan.                  11 = Light Cyan.
              4 = Red.                   12 = Light Red.
              5 = Magenta.               13 = Light Magenta.
              6 = Brown.                 14 = Yellow.
              7 = Light Gray.            15 = White.

            The background color always defaults to black.


Function:   Set color palette.

Header:     PROCEDURE palette(c: integer);

Remarks:    This  procedure is used to select the  color  palette 
            when operating in modes 4 and 5. For all other modes, 
            a  call  to palette has no effect.  c is  an  integer 
            between  0  and 3 which selects the foreground  color 
            palette according to the table below:

              0 = Light Green, Light Red and Yellow colors.
              1 = Light Cyan, Light Magenta and White colors.
              2 = Green, Red and Brown colors.
              3 = Cyan, Magenta and Light Gray colors.

            The default palette is 1. The screen can only display 
            the colors of one palette.  Thus,  all foreground co-
            lors  on  the  screen change instantly  when  an  new 
            palette is selected.


Function:   Set enhanced graphics color palette.

Header:     PROCEDURE egapalette(var p: paletterec);

Remarks:    This  procedure  is used to select the color  palette 
            when operating in modes 13,  14 and 16. For all other 
            modes,  a  call to egapalette has no effect.  p is  a 
            variable  of  type paletterec,  which is  defined  in 
            GRAPHICS.PAS:

              TYPE paletterec =
                RECORD
                  color: ARRAY[0..15] of byte;
                  overscan: byte;
                END;

            The default palette used when crtmode of 13 or 14 
            is called is:
            const
              defaultegapalette1314: PaletteRec=
               (Color:(0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,56,57,58,59,60,61,62,63);
                Overscan:0);

            The default palette used when crtmode of 16 is called 
            is:
            const
              defaultegapalette16: PaletteRec=
               (Color:(0,1,2,3,4,5,20,7,56,57,58,59,60,61,62,63);
                Overscan:0);

            The screen can only display the colors of one  palet-
            te.  Thus,  all colors on the screen change instantly 
            when  an new palette is selected.  The enhanced grap-
            hics adapter can display 16 colors at one time from a 
            palette of 64.  A color can be use for more than  one 
            palette  register,  enabling you to make quick screen 
            update.  One  way  of doing this is  by  selecting  a 
            palette  of  16 times color 0,  make the drawing  and 
            when finished set the palette back to  default,  for-
            cing the drawing to be visible.

            The  Overscan color is what will be displayed on  the 
            border  of  the screen,  but only in modes  with  200 
            lines (crtmode 3, 4, 13 and 14).


Function:   Set pen color.

Header:     PROCEDURE pencolor(c: integer);

Remarks:    c is an integer between 0 and 1, 0 and 3, or 0 and 15 
            depending  on the current mode.  It selects the fore-
            ground color, i.e. the color to be used for lines and 
            dots drawn by the lineto, lineby, line, plotat, plot-
            by,  arc, circle, writestring  and rectfill routines. 
            For  modes 6,  8,  9,  64 and 72,  c selects a  color 
            according to the table below:

              0 = Black.
              1 = White.

            For modes 4 and 5, c selects a color according to the 
            table below:

              0 = Background color set by background.

              1 = Light Green (palette 0) or 
                  Light Cyan (palette 1) or
                  Green (palette 2) or 
                  Cyan (palette 3).

              2 = Light Red (palette 0) or
                  Light Magenta (palette 1)
                  Red (palette 2)
                  Magenta (palette 3).

              3 = Yellow (palette 0) or
                  White (palette 1) or
                  Brown (palette 2) or
                  Light Gray (palette 3).

            For modes 13,  14 and 16, c selects a color according 
            to the table below:

              0 = Black.                  8 = Dark Gray.
              1 = Blue.                   9 = Light Blue.
              2 = Green.                 10 = Light Green.
              3 = Cyan.                  11 = Light Cyan.
              4 = Red.                   12 = Light Red.
              5 = Magenta.               13 = Light Magenta.
              6 = Brown.                 14 = Yellow.
              7 = Light Gray.            15 = White.


Function:   Define color mapping.

Header:     PROCEDURE colormode(m: integer);

Remarks:    When  plotting  the  current pencolor can be  use  to 
            modify the screen in four different ways according to 
            the colormode:

              0 = Replace with color
              1 = AND color with screen contents
              2 = OR color with screen contents
              3 = XOR color with screen contents

            Mode 0 is default and will be used when color  repla-
            cing is wanted.


Function:   Fill screen.

Header:     PROCEDURE fillscreen(c: integer);

Remarks:    Fillscreen  fills  the entire window with  the  color 
            specified by c. c is an integer with the same defini-
            tion  of the colors associated with it as for  penco-
            lor,  please  refer  to the description of  pencolor. 
            When  using colormode in different  modes  fillscreen 
            can make some dramatic effects.  F.ex. when colormode 
            is  set for exor mode the screen can be  inverted  by 
            inverting  some of the colorbits.  When colormode  is 
            set  for 'and' or 'or' mode colorbits can be subtrac-
            ted or added from the screen.


Function:   Set aspect factor.

Header:     PROCEDURE aspect(aspectx,aspecty: integer);

Remarks:    Setup aspect factor to be used for circle,  arc, line 
            and move procedures.  Default aspect ratio is 1:1. An 
            aspect  ratio  of 6:5 (aspect(6,5)) in  medium  reso-
            lution or 12:5 in high resolution will normaly make a 
            nicer  looking drawing.  When talking about  distance 
            (line  length  or  circle  radius),   the  unmodified 
            distance  is  used  for  the  coordinate  having  the 
            greatest aspect factor, while the other coordinate is 
            scaled.  Assume  that  aspecty is less than  aspectx, 
            then  a  circle with radius R will be  drawn  with  a 
            width of 2*R and a height of 2*R*aspecty/aspectx.


Function:   Move turtle home.

Header:     PROCEDURE turtlehome;

Remarks:    The  turtle is moved to center if the current window, 
            i.e.  turtlex becomes (x2-x1+1)/2 and turtley becomes 
            (y2-y1+1)/2, where (x1,y1) and (x2,y2) is the parame-
            ters from the last window call.  The turtle is turned 
            to  direction 0.  turtlehome is usefull  for  getting 
            information  about the current window  size.  Suppose 
            the  window always is sized in even steps,  then  the 
            following example can give the window size:

                turtlehome;
                windowxsize:=turtlex*2;
                windowysize:=turtley*2;


Function:   Move turtle.

Header:     PROCEDURE moveto(x,y: integer);

Remarks:    The turtle is moved to (x,y).


Function:   Move turtle relative.

Header:     PROCEDURE moveby(dx,dy: integer);

Remarks:    The  turtle is moved by (dx,dy) relative to its  cur-
            rent position.


Function:   Move turtle by distance.

Header:     PROCEDURE move(dist: integer);

Remarks:    The turtle is moved the distance given by dist in the 
            current direction. If dist is positive, the turtle is 
            moved forwards.  If dist is negative,  the turtle  is 
            moved backwards.

            Note that the declaration of this procedure  prevents 
            you  from using PolyPascal's built-in move procedure. 
            Should  you wish to use both the Turtlegraphics  move 
            procedure  and  the PolyPascal move  procedure  in  a 
            program, then change the move identifier in the GRAP-
            HICS.PAS file to something else, e.g. movedist.


Function:   Draw line.

Header:     PROCEDURE lineto(x,y: integer);

Remarks:    The turtle is moved to (x,y). While it moves it draws 
            a line using the current pen color.


Function:   Draw line relative.

Header:     PROCEDURE lineby(dx,dy: integer);

Remarks:    The  turtle is moved by (dx,dy) relative to its  cur-
            rent position.  While it moves it draws a line  using 
            the current pen color.


Function:   Draw line by distance.

Header:     PROCEDURE line(dist: integer);

Remarks:    The turtle is moved the distance given by dist in the 
            current  direction.  While  it moves it draws a  line 
            using the current pen color.


Function:   Turn turtle absolute.

Header:     PROCEDURE turnto(angle: integer);

Remarks:    The turtle is turned to the specified angle. If angle 
            is less than zero or greater than or equal to 360, it 
            is  converted into a value between 0 and  359.  0  is 
            exactly  to the right,  and counterclockwise rotation 
            represents increasing angles.


Function:   Turn turtle relative.

Header:     PROCEDURE turn(angle: integer);

Remarks:    The  turtle is turned angle degrees from its  current 
            direction.  Positive values turn the turtle  counter-
            clockwise. The new current direction is adjusted to a 
            value between 0 and 359.


Function:   Plot point.

Header:     PROCEDURE plotat(x,y: integer);

Remarks:    Plots a point in the current pen color at (x,y).  The 
            turtle is not moved.


Function:   Plot point relative.

Header:     PROCEDURE plotby(dx,dy: integer);

Remarks:    Plots  a point at the current position  plus  (dx,dy) 
            using the current pen color. The turtle is not moved.


Function:   Paint area.

Header:     PROCEDURE paint(x,y,fc,bc: integer);

Remarks:    Fills in an area on the screen with the color  speci-
            fied  by  fc.  (x,y) are the coordinates of  a  point 
            within  the  area to be painted.  bc is the  boundary 
            color, i.e. the color of the edges of the area. Paint 
            has  no effect if the starting point already has  the 
            boundary  color.  If the shape to be painted  is  not 
            closed, the foreground color will "leak out" and fill 
            the entire window. On the other hand, if the shape is 
            closed  but the starting point is outside  it,  paint 
            will  fill the surrounding area.  Paint never  paints 
            beyond  the current window.  Paint will always assume 
            that colormode is set for replace mode.


Function:   Draw arc.

Header:     PROCEDURE arc(angle,radius: integer);

Remarks:    The  turtle is moved in an arc of the specified angle 
            with the specified radius.  The turtle always  starts 
            off  in the current direction.  If angle is positive, 
            it  turns left (counterclockwise) while  drawing  the 
            arc. If the angle is negative, it turns right (clock-
            wise).


Function:   Draw circle.

Header:     PROCEDURE circle(x,y,radius: integer);

Remarks:    Draws  a  circle.  (x,y) are the coordinates  of  the 
            center of the circle,  and radius is the radius.  The 
            circle  is drawn using the current pen color and  the 
            current aspect ratio. Please refer to the description 
            of  aspect.   The turtle's position and direction are 
            not affected.



Function:   Define fill pattern.

Header:     PROCEDURE pattern(VAR p);

Remarks:    This  procedure  is used to define the  fill  pattern 
            used by the rectfill routine.  rectfill does not just 
            fill all pixels with a given color.  Instead it loads 
            a  user defined pattern into the specified area.  The 
            pattern  is an 8 x 8 dot matrix,  and it  is  defined 
            through a call to pattern. The p parameter must be an 
            array variable with 8 bytes. Each byte corresponds to 
            a  horizontal line in the pattern,  and each bit cor-
            responds to a pixel.  When rectfill fills an area, it 
            actually fills it with 8 x 8 pattern matrices, except 
            for  the  borders,  where appropriate slices  of  the 
            pattern are used. Below follows some examples of fill 
            patterns and their hex equivalents:

              ::OO::::::OO::::    $44          Slanted lines.
              OO::::::OO::::::    $88
              ::::::OO::::::OO    $11
              ::::OO::::::OO::    $22
              ::OO::::::OO::::    $44
              OO::::::OO::::::    $88
              ::::::OO::::::OO    $11
              ::::OO::::::OO::    $22

              OO::OO::OO::OO::    $AA          Grey pattern.
              ::OO::OO::OO::OO    $55
              OO::OO::OO::OO::    $AA
              ::OO::OO::OO::OO    $55
              OO::OO::OO::OO::    $AA
              ::OO::OO::OO::OO    $55
              OO::OO::OO::OO::    $AA
              ::OO::OO::OO::OO    $55

              ::::::::::::OOOO    $03          Cross hatch.
              OO::::::::OO::::    $84
              ::OO::::OO::::::    $48
              ::::OOOO::::::::    $30
              ::::OOOO::::::::    $30
              ::OO::::OO::::::    $48
              OO::::::::OO::::    $84
              ::::::::::::OOOO    $03

            To define the "Cross hatch" pattern in a program, the 
            following construct may be used:

              CONST
                cross: ARRAY[0..7] OF byte =
                ($03,$84,$48,$30,$30,$48,$84,$03);

            To activate the pattern,  simply pass the cross iden-
            tifier to the pattern routine.


Function:   Fill rectangle with pattern.

Header:     PROCEDURE rectfill(x1,y1,x2,y2: integer);

Remarks:    Rectfill fills an area on the screen with the pattern 
            defined  by the last call to the  pattern  procedure. 
            The  pattern is replicated both horizontally and ver-
            tically to fill the entire area.  Bits of value  zero 
            will not be written,  whereas bits of value 1 will be 
            written  using  the current  pencolor.  The  turtle's 
            position and direction are not affected.


Function:   Get picture image.

Header:     PROCEDURE getpic(VAR buffer; x1,y1,x2,y2: integer);

Remarks:    Getpic  transfers the contents of a rectangular  area 
            on  the  graphics screen into a buffer.  The area  is 
            defined by its opposite corners (x1,y1) and  (x2,y2). 
            Buffer is a variable of any type.  The minimum buffer 
            size  in bytes required to store the image is  calcu-
            lated from:

            Four color mode (crtmode 4 and 5):

              size = ((width+3) DIV 4)*height+6

            Two color mode (crtmode 6, 8, 9, 64 and 72):

              size = ((width+7) DIV 8)*height+6

            Sixteen color mode (crtmode 13, 14, 15 and 16):

              size = ((width+1) DIV 2)*height+6

            where:

              width = abs(x1-x2)+1  and  height = abs(y1-y2)+1

            Note  that it is up to the programmer to insure  that 
            the  buffer is large enough to accomodate the  entire 
            transfer.  The first 6 bytes of the buffer constitute 
            a  three  word header  (three  integers).  After  the 
            transfer,  the  first word contains 1,  2 or 4 giving 
            the bitcount pr. pixel. The second word  contains the 
            width  of  the  image and  third  word  contains  the 
            height. The remaining bytes contain the data. Data is 
            stored   with   the  leftmost  pixels  in  the   most 
            significant  bits of the bytes.  At the end  of  each 
            row, the remaining bits of the last byte are skipped. 
            The turtle's position and direction are not affected.


Function:   Put picture image.

Header:     PROCEDURE putpic(VAR buffer; x,y: integer);

Remarks:    Putpic  transfers a picture image from a buffer  onto 
            the screen. (x,y) define the lower left corner of the 
            rectangular picture area. buffer is a variable of any 
            type,  which has been initialized by a previous  call 
            to  getpic.  Each bit in the buffer is converted to a 
            color according to the colormode before it is written 
            to  the screen.  The turtle's position and  direction 
            are not affected.


Function:   Return color of dot.

Header:     FUNCTION point(x,y: integer): integer;

Remarks:    Returns the color of the dot at (x,y),  i.e.  0-3 for 
            320 x 200 graphics,  0-1 for 640 x 200 graphics or 0-
            15  for enhanced graphics.  If (x,y) is  outside  the 
            window, point returns -1.


Function:   Return turtle x-coordinate.

Header:     FUNCTION turtlex: integer;

Remarks:    Returns the turtle's x-coordinate.


Function:   Return turtle y-coordinate.

Header:     FUNCTION turtley: integer;

Remarks:    Returns the turtle's y-coordinate.


Function:   Return turtle angle.

Header:     FUNCTION turtleang: integer;

Remarks:    Returns  the direction in which the turtle is moving. 
            The returned angle is always between 0 and 359.


Function:   Set working page

Header:     PROCEDURE workpage(p: integer);

Remarks:    Set  working  page  to page p.  If page p is  to  big 
            nothing happens.


Function:   Set display page

Header:     PROCEDURE disppage(p: integer);

Remarks:    Set  display  page  to page p.  If page p is  to  big 
            nothing happens.


Function:   Copy from page to page


Header:     PROCEDURE copypage(p1,p2: integer);

Remarks:    Copy  from screen buffer p1 to screen buffer  p2.  If 
            either p1 or p2 is to big no copying is done.


Function:   Write text to graphics screen.

Header:     PROCEDURE writestring(s: str_80);

Remarks:    s is a string expression of up to 80 characters.  The 
            string  is placed on the screen with left side of the 
            baseline  on the current turtlex position.  The base-
            line is the line on which the characters are  placed. 
            Some  characters  like  'g' will be drawn  below  the 
            baseline. The current X and Y position is incremented 
            for each character in the string according to what is 
            defined with the charfont procedure. writestring uses 
            the character style and size defined by the last call 
            to  charstyle,  charsize  or  charfont.  The  default 
            graphics  font  used  is set for  8x8  characters  in 
            normal style and with a size of one.  Other fonts can 
            be installed, see the charfont procedure.


Function:   Set character style

Header:     PROCEDURE charstyle(Style: INTEGER);

Remarks:    Set character style for normal or italic:

                Style=0: Normal.
                Style=1: Italic

            Selecting  normal  style will force characters to  be 
            drawn  within a rectangle,  while italic  will  force 
            each  second  line of the character to be  moved  one 
            position  to the right above the baseline and to  the 
            left below the baseline.


Function:   Set character size

Header:     PROCEDURE charsize(Size: INTEGER);

Remark:     Size is a scaling factor for each character.  If size 
            is  two,  characters will be drawn with double height 
            and width etc.


Function:   Set character font, style and size

Header:     PROCEDURE charfont(Var Font);

Remark:     The  default  graphics font used is  located  in  the 
            BIOS-ROM  and  is  set for 8x8  charactes  in  normal 
            style.  Other  fonts  can  be  installed  using  this 
            procedure.  Font  is a record defined in GRAPHICS.PAS 
            as:

            RECORD
              Table: ^byte;     { Pointer to Font $F000:$FA6E }
              Bitoffset,        { bit offset                0 }
              Bitcount,         { bit/character            64 }
              Width,            { character width           8 }
              Height,           { height                    8 }
              Ascent,           { topline-baseline          7 }
              Size,             { scaling factor            1 }
              Style,            { 0=normal, 1=Italic        0 }
              DeltaX,           { turtle increment X        8 }
              DeltaY: integer); { turtle increment Y        0 }
            END;

            The default font can be set with:

            charfont(romfont);

            When  charfont is called,  style and size is  initia-
            lized.

            Bitcount is the number of bits for each character  in 
            the  font definition.  Bitcount will normaly be equal 
            to  width*height,  but can be different in  order  to 
            make  each  character definition start  on  byte/word 
            boundary.

            Bitoffset  is the number of bits to skip,  normaly 0. 
            If  the first character defined in the  fonttable  is 
            for a space (chr(32)) then bitoffset will be 32 times 
            bitcount.

            Width and height are the size of the box in which the 
            character will be drawn.

            Ascent is the number of lines above the baseline. The 
            baseline has the y coordinate turtley.

            The file GRAP9X14.FON on the diskette contains a 9 by 
            14  character  set  (the one used  for  the  Hercules 
            graphics card). The next example shows a procedure to 
            install this font:

            PROCEDURE initfont9x14;
              PROCEDURE f9x14; EXTERNAL 'grap9x14.fon';
            var font9x14: font;

            BEGIN
              WITH font9x14 do begin
                table:=ptr(cseg,ofs(f9x14));
                bitoffset:=0;   { first character is chr(0) }
                bitcount:=8*16; { 8x16 }
                width:=8;       { Size 9x14 }
                height:=14;
                ascent:=11;     { 11 lines from top to baseline }
                size:=1;        { Small }
                style:=0;       { Normal style }
                deltax:=9;      { Increment turtle position }
                deltay:=0;      { by (9,0) }
              END;
              charfont(font9x14);
            END;

            When including the file GRAP9X14.FON in your program, 
            4 Kbytes of your program space will be used to  store 
            the data.

            To  change  the character fonts you  can  modify  the 
            GRAP9X14.FON file. This file contains a definition of 
            the  entire  IBM PC character set  (256  characters). 
            Each character occupies 16 bytes,  but only the first 
            14 are used. The first byte of a character definition 
            corresponds to the top scan line, and the most signi-
            ficant  bit  in  a byte corresponds to  the  leftmost 
            pixel.  To find the address at which a specific char-
            acter definition is stored in the file, simply multi-
            ply its ASCII value by 16.
